notes.body:
C++ is a compiled language. You write source code in a text file (for example main.cpp), a compiler translates it into machine code, and the operating system runs the resulting executable. ELEC40004 assumes no previous programming, so the first weeks are about getting comfortable with this cycle: edit, compile, read the errors, fix, run, test.

The build pipeline
- Preprocessor: handles lines starting with #, such as #include <iostream>, which pastes in the declarations for the standard input/output library.
- Compiler: checks syntax and types and produces object code. Most of your early errors (missing semicolon, undeclared variable, type mismatch) are compile-time errors.
- Linker: joins your object code with library code to make the executable. "Undefined reference" errors come from here, usually because a function was declared but never defined.
- A typical command line: g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -o hello hello.cpp, then ./hello to run it. -Wall turns on warnings; treat warnings as bugs waiting to happen.

A first program
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name;
    int age;
    std::cout << "Name and age: ";
    std::cin >> name >> age;
    std::cout << "Hello " << name << ", next year you will be " << age + 1 << std::endl;
    return 0;
}

Key points
- Every program has exactly one main function; execution starts there. Returning 0 signals success to the operating system.
- std::cout is the standard output stream and << is the insertion operator. std::cin is standard input and >> is the extraction operator.
- >> skips leading whitespace and reads one "word" for a string, so typing "Ada Lovelace 36" would put "Ada" in name and then fail to read "Lovelace" as an int. Use std::getline(std::cin, line) to read a whole line.
- After a failed read, the stream goes into a fail state and further reads do nothing until you clear it. Checking if (std::cin >> x) is the standard way to detect bad input.
- std:: is the namespace prefix for the standard library. Many courses write it out in full rather than using "using namespace std;", which can cause name clashes in larger programs.

Kinds of error
- Compile-time: the compiler refuses to build the program. Read the first error message first; later ones are often knock-on effects.
- Run-time: the program builds but crashes (for example dereferencing a null pointer or reading outside an array).
- Logic: the program runs but gives the wrong answer. Only testing catches these.

Testing and debugging
The module's learning outcomes include verifying behaviour with test benches and diagnosing faults. In practice:
- Write small test inputs whose correct output you know by hand, including edge cases (zero, negative numbers, empty input, the largest allowed value).
- Print intermediate values, or use a debugger (such as gdb or the one built into your IDE) to step through the code and watch variables.
- Change one thing at a time and re-test.

Exam tips: be able to explain what the preprocessor, compiler and linker each do, predict exactly what a short I/O program prints for a given input, and say how you would test it.

Term 1 of ELEC40004 is about core imperative programming: storing values in variables, making decisions, repeating work and breaking a program into functions.

Variables and types
- A variable is a named, typed piece of memory: int count = 0; double v = 3.3; bool ok = true; char c = 'A'; std::string s = "hi";
- int holds whole numbers (typically 32 bits); double holds floating-point numbers; bool holds true/false.
- Integer division truncates towards zero: 7 / 2 is 3 and 7 % 2 is 1, but 7 / 2.0 is 3.5 because one operand is a double.
- Floating-point values are approximations, so compare them with a tolerance (std::abs(a - b) < 1e-9) rather than ==.
- A variable's scope is the block { } in which it is declared; it does not exist outside it.

Control flow
- if / else if / else chooses between alternatives. A common bug is writing if (x = 5), which assigns rather than compares.
- while (condition) { } repeats while the condition is true; it may run zero times.
- do { } while (condition); always runs at least once (useful for input validation).
- for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { } is the standard counting loop: initialise, test, update.
- break leaves the innermost loop; continue skips to the next iteration.
- switch selects on an integer or char value; remember break at the end of each case, or execution falls through to the next.

Functions
A function packages a task with a name, parameters and a return type. It must be declared before it is used (or defined above main).

bool is_prime(int n) {
    if (n < 2) {
        return false;
    }
    for (int d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) {
        if (n % d == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

Calling is_prime for i from 1 to 20 prints 2 3 5 7 11 13 17 19, eight primes. Testing only up to d * d <= n works because any factor larger than √n pairs with one smaller than √n.

Passing parameters
- Pass by value (int n): the function gets a copy; changes do not affect the caller.
- Pass by reference (int& n): the function works on the caller's variable, so it can modify it.
- Pass by const reference (const std::string& s): no copy and no modification; the normal choice for large objects you only read.

void swap_ints(int& a, int& b) {
    int tmp = a;
    a = b;
    b = tmp;
}

With x = 1 and y = 2, swap_ints(x, y) leaves x = 2 and y = 1. If the parameters were plain int, the swap would happen to copies and x and y would be unchanged.

Other points
- A void function returns nothing.
- Overloading: two functions can share a name if their parameter lists differ.
- Keep functions short and single-purpose; this makes testing each one on its own (unit testing) straightforward.

Exam tips: trace loops by hand with a small table of variable values per iteration; watch for off-by-one errors, integer division and = versus ==.

Engineering programs usually work on data that lives in files: logged sensor readings, component lists, netlists or test vectors. A learning outcome of ELEC40004 is designing programs that use file-based I/O and communicate in a text-based environment.

File streams
- #include <fstream> gives std::ifstream (input file stream) and std::ofstream (output file stream).
- They behave like std::cin and std::cout, so >>, << and std::getline all work.
- Always check that the file opened: if (!infile.is_open()) { ... } because a wrong path is the most common failure.
- An ofstream opened on an existing file truncates it by default. Use std::ofstream f("log.txt", std::ios::app); to append.
- Streams close automatically when they go out of scope, but calling close() explicitly is fine.

The standard read loop
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::ifstream infile("readings.txt");
    if (!infile.is_open()) {
        std::cout << "error opening file" << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }
    double x;
    double sum = 0;
    int n = 0;
    while (infile >> x) {
        sum += x;
        n++;
    }
    infile.close();

    std::ofstream outfile("summary.txt");
    outfile << "count " << n << std::endl;
    if (n > 0) {
        outfile << "mean " << sum / n << std::endl;
    }
    outfile.close();
}

With readings.txt containing 3.5, 4.0, 2.5 and 6.0 on separate lines, summary.txt contains "count 4" and "mean 4".

Why test the read itself?
The expression infile >> x evaluates to the stream, which converts to false once a read fails (end of file or bad data). Writing while (!infile.eof()) is a classic bug: eof only becomes true once a read has tried to go past the end of the file. If the file ends with a newline (the usual case), reading the last value does not set eof, so the loop runs once more, that read fails, and the last value is processed twice.

Line-based parsing
When each line is a record, read whole lines and then split them with a string stream:

std::string line;
while (std::getline(infile, line)) {
    std::stringstream ss(line);
    std::string id;
    double ohms;
    if (ss >> id >> ohms) {
        std::cout << id << " -> " << ohms << std::endl;
    }
}

This needs #include <sstream>. Reading line by line makes it easy to skip blank or malformed lines without losing your place in the file.

Text-based communication
Many programs read from standard input and write to standard output so they can be chained with other tools or driven by a test harness. You can redirect files at the shell: ./prog < input.txt > output.txt. Writing output in a precise, predictable format matters, because automated tests compare it character by character.

Good practice
- Validate input: check ranges and reject impossible values (negative resistance, missing fields).
- Separate reading, processing and writing into different functions.
- Keep the data in a container such as std::vector once read, rather than re-reading the file.

Exam tips: know the while (file >> x) idiom and why the eof-controlled loop is wrong, how to open for append, and how getline plus stringstream parses a line into fields.

The Standard Template Library (STL) is the part of the C++ standard library that provides ready-made containers, iterators and algorithms. A learning outcome of ELEC40004 is choosing STL data structures on the basis of performance and functionality and understanding how they are implemented underneath.

Containers you must know
- std::vector<T>: a dynamic array. Elements are contiguous in memory, so v[i] is O(1). push_back is amortised O(1) because the vector grows its capacity geometrically when full. Inserting or erasing at the front or middle is O(n) because later elements must shift.
- std::list<T>: a doubly linked list. Insertion and removal anywhere are O(1) once you have an iterator to the position, but there is no indexing and finding an element is O(n).
- std::map<K, V>: keys kept in sorted order; lookup, insertion and removal are O(log n). It is normally implemented as a balanced binary search tree (typically a red-black tree).
- std::unordered_map<K, V>: a hash table; average O(1) lookup, no ordering of keys.
- std::set<T> and std::unordered_set<T>: as above but storing keys only, without duplicates.
- std::string behaves much like a vector of char with extra text operations.

Iterators
An iterator is an object that points to an element in a container and can be moved to the next one. v.begin() points to the first element and v.end() points one past the last, so the half-open range [begin, end) covers the whole container. Range-based for loops (for (int x : v)) use iterators behind the scenes.

Algorithms
#include <algorithm> provides generic functions that work on iterator ranges:
- std::sort(v.begin(), v.end()) sorts in O(n log n).
- std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), value) returns an iterator to the first match, or end() if absent.
- std::count, std::max_element, std::reverse and many others.

A short example
std::vector<int> v = {5, 2, 9, 1};
v.push_back(7);
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
// v is now 1 2 5 7 9, size 5

std::map<std::string, int> counts;
std::vector<std::string> words = {"bit", "byte", "bit", "word", "bit"};
for (const std::string& w : words) {
    counts[w]++;
}
// iterating counts gives bit: 3, byte: 1, word: 1 (sorted by key)

auto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 9);
// it - v.begin() is 4

Notes on the example
- counts[w] creates the entry with value 0 if w is new, so ++ works first time. Use counts.find(w) or counts.count(w) if you only want to test membership without inserting.
- Because v was sorted, 9 is at index 4.

Choosing a container
- Mostly appending and indexing: vector (the sensible default).
- Fast lookup by key, keys needed in order: map. Order not needed: unordered_map.
- Frequent insertion and removal in the middle while iterating: list may help, although vector is often faster in practice for small sizes because of cache locality.

Pitfalls
- v[i] does no bounds checking; v.at(i) throws std::out_of_range if i is invalid.
- push_back can reallocate, which invalidates existing iterators, pointers and references into the vector.
- v.size() returns an unsigned type, so for (int i = 0; i < v.size() - 1; i++) misbehaves on an empty vector because 0 - 1 wraps round to a huge number.

Exam tips: give big-O costs for the main operations of vector, list and map and justify a container choice for a stated task.

Real data comes in groups: a point has an x and a y; a resistor has an identifier and a value; a sample has a time and a voltage. A struct lets you bundle related values of different types into one new type, which makes code clearer and lets you pass one object instead of many separate variables.

Defining and using a struct
#include <cmath>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};

double distance(const Point& a, const Point& b) {
    double dx = a.x - b.x;
    double dy = a.y - b.y;
    return std::sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
}

int main() {
    std::vector<Point> path = {{0, 0}, {3, 4}, {6, 8}};
    double total = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i < (int)path.size(); i++) {
        total += distance(path[i - 1], path[i]);
    }
    std::cout << "path length " << total << std::endl;
}

Each leg is a 3-4-5 triangle, so the program prints path length 10.

Key ideas
- The struct definition creates a new type; it must end with a semicolon after the closing brace.
- Members are accessed with the dot operator: p.x. Through a pointer you use the arrow operator: ptr->x, which means (*ptr).x.
- Aggregate initialisation with braces fills members in order: Point p = {3, 4};
- Structs can be copied and assigned as a whole; the copy is member by member.
- Structs can be nested (a Line containing two Points) and stored in containers (std::vector<Point>).
- Pass structs to functions by const reference when the function only reads them, to avoid copying.

struct versus class
In C++ a struct and a class are almost identical. The only language difference is the default access: members (and base classes) of a struct are public by default, those of a class are private by default. By convention, struct is used for simple passive data with no invariants to protect, and class for types that hide their data behind member functions (the next topic).

Arrays of structs versus parallel arrays
Beginners sometimes keep std::vector<std::string> names and std::vector<double> values side by side, relying on matching indices. A single std::vector of structs is safer: sorting or deleting cannot make the fields drift out of step.

Sorting a vector of structs
std::sort needs to know how to compare two structs. You can supply a comparison function:

bool by_value(const Resistor& a, const Resistor& b) {
    return a.ohms < b.ohms;
}
std::sort(rs.begin(), rs.end(), by_value);

The comparison must define a strict ordering (use < not <=).

Enumerations
A related structured type is enum class, which defines a type with a fixed set of named values, for example enum class Colour { Red, Green, Blue }; This is clearer and safer than using magic numbers such as 0, 1 and 2.

Reading structs from a file
Structs fit naturally with file input: while (infile >> r.id >> r.ohms) { rs.push_back(r); } reads one record per iteration into a struct and stores a copy.

Exam tips: write struct definitions correctly (including the final semicolon), know dot versus arrow, and explain why a vector of structs beats parallel arrays.

Term 2 of ELEC40004 moves from writing functions to designing larger software, and the main tool is the class. A learning outcome is using encapsulation and abstraction to manage software complexity.

Definitions
- Abstraction: presenting what an object does (its interface) while hiding how it does it. A user of std::vector calls push_back without knowing about capacity and reallocation.
- Encapsulation: bundling data with the functions that operate on it and restricting direct access to the data, so the object can protect its own consistency.
- Invariant: a condition that must always hold for a valid object, for example 0 ≤ minutes ≤ 59 for a clock time. Encapsulation lets the class guarantee it.

A class with an invariant
class Time {
public:
    Time(int h, int m) {
        set(h, m);
    }
    void add_minutes(int extra) {
        set(hours, mins + extra);
    }
    int get_hours() const { return hours; }
    int get_mins() const { return mins; }

private:
    int hours;
    int mins;
    void set(int h, int m) {
        int total = ((h * 60 + m) % 1440 + 1440) % 1440;
        hours = total / 60;
        mins = total % 60;
    }
};

Time t(23, 50); t.add_minutes(25); leaves the time at 0:15. Because hours and mins are private, no outside code can set mins to 75; every change goes through set, which normalises the value (the extra + 1440 handles negative inputs, since % can return a negative result in C++).

Parts of a class
- public members form the interface; private members are implementation details visible only inside the class.
- A constructor has the class name and no return type; it runs when an object is created and should establish the invariant. A member initialiser list, such as Rectangle(double w, double h) : w(w), h(h) {}, initialises members directly.
- A const member function (int get_hours() const) promises not to modify the object, so it can be called on const objects.
- A destructor (~ClassName()) runs when the object is destroyed; it releases resources such as dynamic memory or open files.
- Member functions can be defined inside the class or outside using the scope operator: void Time::add_minutes(int extra) { ... }.
- In larger programs the class definition goes in a header (.hpp/.h) and member definitions in a source file (.cpp).

Why encapsulate?
- Safety: invalid states become impossible rather than merely discouraged.
- Change: you can change the internal representation (say, store total minutes instead of hours and minutes) without changing any code that uses the class.
- Reasoning: you only need to check the class's own member functions to know the invariant always holds.

Operator overloading
Classes can define operators so they behave like built-in types, for example bool operator<(const Time& other) const for sorting, or operator<< to print with std::cout. Overload only where the meaning is obvious.

Modelling real-world domains
The module also asks you to model domains with object-oriented design. A good approach is to identify the nouns in the problem (component, circuit, node, sensor) as candidate classes, the verbs as member functions, and the rules that must always be true as invariants.

Exam tips: distinguish abstraction from encapsulation in one sentence each, state the struct/class default-access difference, and show how a private member plus a validating setter enforces an invariant.

C++ lets you work very close to the hardware, which is one reason the module uses it for electrical and computer engineers. Pointers and manual memory management are where that power, and most beginner bugs, come from.

Built-in arrays
- int arr[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4}; is a fixed-size block of four ints laid out contiguously in memory. Indices run from 0 to 3.
- The size must be a compile-time constant and cannot change.
- There is no bounds checking: arr[4] is undefined behaviour and may silently corrupt other data.
- When passed to a function, an array decays to a pointer to its first element, so the function does not know its length; pass the length separately or use std::vector or std::array.

Pointers
- A pointer stores a memory address. int* p = &a; makes p point to a (& is the address-of operator).
- *p dereferences the pointer, giving the object it points to, so *p = 20; changes a to 20.
- nullptr is the null pointer value; dereferencing it is undefined behaviour (usually a crash).
- Pointer arithmetic moves in units of the pointed-to type: if q points to arr[0], then q + 2 points to arr[2] and *(q + 2) equals q[2].

int a = 10;
int* p = &a;
*p = 20;               // a is now 20
int arr[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
int* q = arr;          // array decays to pointer to arr[0]
// *(q + 2) is 3 and q[3] is 4

References versus pointers
A reference (int& r = a;) is an alias that must be initialised and cannot be re-seated or null. A pointer can be null, can be changed to point elsewhere, and needs explicit dereferencing.

Stack and heap
- Local variables live on the stack and are destroyed automatically at the end of their scope.
- new allocates an object on the heap (free store) and returns a pointer; the object lives until you delete it.
- Arrays allocated with new[] must be freed with delete[]; mixing new[] with plain delete is undefined behaviour.

int n = 5;
double* data = new double[n];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    data[i] = i * 0.5;
}
// data[4] is 2
delete[] data;
data = nullptr;

Here n need not be known until run time, which is the main reason for dynamic allocation.

Classic memory bugs
- Memory leak: losing the last pointer to heap memory without deleting it. Long-running programs gradually run out of memory.
- Dangling pointer: using a pointer after the memory it points to has been deleted, or returning the address of a local variable.
- Double delete: deleting the same memory twice.
- Buffer overrun: writing past the end of an array.

Modern practice
Prefer std::vector to new[] and smart pointers to raw new/delete: std::unique_ptr<T> (from <memory>) deletes its object automatically when it goes out of scope (RAII: resource acquisition is initialisation). You still need raw pointers to understand how containers, linked structures and hardware registers work, which is why the module teaches them.

Exam tips: be able to trace pointer code and draw memory diagrams with boxes and arrows; know &, *, ->, new/delete versus new[]/delete[], and name and explain leaks and dangling pointers.

Using the STL is not enough for ELEC40004: the syllabus also asks you to implement data structures such as vectors, lists and trees, so that you understand why each has the performance it does.

A growable array (how vector works)
A home-made vector keeps three things: a pointer to a heap array, the number of elements in use (size) and the number of slots allocated (capacity). To push_back when size == capacity:
- allocate a new array of, say, double the capacity;
- copy the existing elements across;
- delete[] the old array and point at the new one;
- then store the new element.
Doubling makes the total copying over n insertions at most about 2n element copies, so the amortised cost per insertion is O(1).

Singly linked list
Each node holds a value and a pointer to the next node; the list is just a pointer to the first node (head), with nullptr marking the end.

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
};

Node* push_front(Node* head, int v) {
    return new Node{v, head};
}

void print_list(const Node* head) {
    for (const Node* cur = head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->next) {
        std::cout << cur->value << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
}

void free_list(Node* head) {
    while (head != nullptr) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        delete head;
        head = next;
    }
}

Pushing 1, 2 and 3 to the front and printing gives 3 2 1. Notice that free_list saves next before deleting the node; reading head->next after delete head would use a dangling pointer.

Costs: insertion or removal at the head is O(1); finding the k-th element or searching is O(n) because you must walk the chain. A doubly linked list (like std::list) adds a prev pointer so you can also remove a node in O(1) given a pointer to it.

Binary search trees
A binary tree node has a key and two child pointers. In a binary search tree (BST), every key in a node's left subtree is smaller than the node's key and every key in its right subtree is larger.

TreeNode* insert(TreeNode* t, int k) {
    if (t == nullptr) {
        return new TreeNode{k, nullptr, nullptr};
    }
    if (k < t->key) {
        t->left = insert(t->left, k);
    } else if (k > t->key) {
        t->right = insert(t->right, k);
    }
    return t;
}

void in_order(const TreeNode* t) {
    if (t == nullptr) {
        return;
    }
    in_order(t->left);
    std::cout << t->key << " ";
    in_order(t->right);
}

Inserting 8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14 and printing in order gives 1 3 6 8 10 14: an in-order traversal of a BST always visits keys in sorted order. Duplicates are ignored here, giving set behaviour.

Performance of BSTs
- Search, insert and delete take time proportional to the height of the tree.
- A balanced tree with n nodes has height about log₂ n, so operations are O(log n).
- Inserting keys in sorted order (1, 2, 3, ...) produces a chain with height n (counting nodes on the longest path, as in the exercises here; n − 1 if height counts edges), so operations degrade to O(n). Self-balancing trees (such as the red-black trees behind std::map) avoid this.

Traversals
- Pre-order: node, left, right (useful for copying a tree).
- In-order: left, node, right (sorted output for a BST).
- Post-order: left, right, node (used when deleting a tree, because children must be freed before their parent).

Wrapping in a class
In practice you hide the raw pointers inside a class (e.g. IntList or IntSet) whose destructor frees every node. If such a class can be copied, it needs a proper copy constructor and assignment operator (the "rule of three"), or copying must be disabled; otherwise two objects share nodes and both try to delete them.

Exam tips: draw the pointers before and after each operation, state the big-O of each operation and the worst-case BST shape, and remember to free memory in the right order.

An algorithm is a precise, finite sequence of steps that solves a problem. ELEC40004 covers algorithms alongside recursion, and you are expected to reason about how running time grows with input size.

Big-O notation
Big-O describes how the number of basic steps grows as the input size n grows, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms.
- O(1): constant (array indexing).
- O(log n): halving the problem each step (binary search, balanced tree lookup).
- O(n): one pass over the data (linear search, summing a vector).
- O(n log n): efficient comparison sorting (merge sort; std::sort).
- O(n²): nested loops over the data (bubble, selection or insertion sort in the worst case).
- O(2ⁿ): naive recursive Fibonacci.
For n = 1,000,000, log₂ n is about 20, so binary search needs about 20 comparisons where linear search may need a million.

Recursion
A recursive function calls itself on a smaller version of the problem. Every recursive function needs:
- a base case that is answered directly, without recursion;
- a recursive case that moves strictly closer to the base case.
Without a reachable base case the calls never stop and the program crashes with a stack overflow, because each call uses a new stack frame for its parameters and local variables.

long long factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

factorial(5) unwinds as 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120.

Recursive binary search
Binary search works only on sorted data. Compare the target with the middle element and discard the half that cannot contain it.

int binary_search(const std::vector<int>& v, int target, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo > hi) {
        return -1;
    }
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (v[mid] == target) {
        return mid;
    } else if (v[mid] < target) {
        return binary_search(v, target, mid + 1, hi);
    } else {
        return binary_search(v, target, lo, mid - 1);
    }
}

For v = {2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38}, searching for 23 checks index 3 (12), then index 5 (23) and returns 5; searching for 7 returns -1. Writing lo + (hi - lo) / 2 rather than (lo + hi) / 2 avoids integer overflow when indices are very large.

Recursion versus iteration
- Any recursive algorithm can be written iteratively (sometimes with an explicit stack), and vice versa.
- Recursion is natural for recursively defined structures: trees, divide-and-conquer sorting, expression parsing.
- Iteration avoids function-call overhead and stack depth limits, so a simple loop is better for things like summing a list.

Divide and conquer
Merge sort splits the array in half, sorts each half recursively, then merges the two sorted halves in linear time. The recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) solves to O(n log n). Quicksort partitions around a pivot; it averages O(n log n) but its worst case is O(n²).

The Fibonacci warning
fib(n) = fib(n−1) + fib(n−2) written directly recomputes the same values again and again, giving exponential time. Storing results already computed (memoisation) or using a loop makes it O(n).

Exam tips: always state the base case first, trace small recursive calls with a call tree, and justify big-O by counting how many times the main loop body or recursive call runs.

The final syllabus item is an introduction to inheritance and polymorphism, the features that let object-oriented programs model families of related things, such as different circuit components or shapes, through one common interface.

Inheritance
- class Derived : public Base { ... }; means Derived "is a" Base: it inherits Base's members and can add or override behaviour.
- public members of the base stay public in the derived class; private members exist in the derived object but are not directly accessible to the derived class's code. protected members are accessible to derived classes but not to outside code.
- A derived constructor calls the base constructor in its initialiser list, e.g. Resistor(double r) : Component("R"), r(r) {}.
- Use inheritance for genuine "is a" relationships. For "has a" relationships (a circuit has components) use composition: a member variable.

Virtual functions and polymorphism
A virtual function can be overridden in derived classes, and the version that runs is chosen at run time from the object's actual type, not the type of the pointer or reference used to call it. This is dynamic (run-time) polymorphism.

#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

class Shape {
public:
    virtual ~Shape() {}
    virtual double area() const = 0;
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
public:
    Rectangle(double w, double h) : w(w), h(h) {}
    double area() const override { return w * h; }
private:
    double w, h;
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    Circle(double r) : r(r) {}
    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; }
private:
    double r;
};

int main() {
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Shape>> shapes;
    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique<Rectangle>(2, 3));
    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique<Circle>(1));
    double total = 0;
    for (const auto& s : shapes) {
        total += s->area();
    }
    std::cout << total << std::endl;
}

The loop calls the right area() for each object and prints 9.14159 (6 + 3.14159). The loop never needs to know which concrete shapes exist, so you can add a Triangle class later without changing it.

Key terms
- Pure virtual function: declared with = 0; it normally has no implementation in the base class, and derived classes must override it.
- Abstract class: a class with at least one pure virtual function; you cannot create objects of it, only of derived classes that override all the pure virtual functions.
- override: a keyword (C++11) that asks the compiler to check that the function really overrides a base-class virtual function; it catches typos and mismatched signatures.
- Virtual destructor: if objects are deleted through a base-class pointer, the base destructor must be virtual, otherwise the derived part is not destroyed properly (undefined behaviour).

Why pointers or references?
Polymorphism works through pointers or references to the base class. If you store derived objects by value in a std::vector<Shape> (impossible here because Shape is abstract, but possible with a concrete base), each object is sliced: only the base part is copied and the derived data and overrides are lost. That is why the example stores std::unique_ptr<Shape>, which also deletes the objects automatically.

Static versus dynamic binding
Without virtual, the function called is chosen at compile time from the declared type of the pointer (static binding). With virtual, it is chosen at run time through a table of function pointers (the vtable), at a small cost per call.

Design advice
Program to the interface: write functions that take const Shape& or Shape* so they work for every current and future derived class. Keep base classes small, and prefer composition when the relationship is not truly "is a".

Exam tips: predict which function is called with and without virtual, define abstract class and pure virtual function precisely, and explain slicing and why base classes need virtual destructors.

