};

double time_of_peak(const std::vector<Sample>& s) {
    int best = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i < (int)s.size(); i++) {
        if (s[i].v > s[best].v) {
            best = i;
        }
    }
    return s[best].t;
}
The function assumes the vector is not empty (the caller should check, or the function could signal an error). Passing by const reference avoids copying a potentially large vector on every call, and the const means the compiler will stop the function from accidentally changing the caller's data.

class Battery {
public:
    Battery(double capacity_mAh) : capacity(capacity_mAh > 0 ? capacity_mAh : 1), charge(capacity) {}
    void draw(double current_mA, double hours) {
        if (current_mA < 0 || hours < 0) { return; }
        charge -= current_mA * hours;
        if (charge < 0) { charge = 0; }
    }
    double get_charge() const { return charge; }
    double percent() const { return 100 * charge / capacity; }
private:
    double capacity;
    double charge;
};
capacity and charge are private, so no outside code can set them directly. The constructor makes the battery start full with a positive capacity, which establishes 0 ≤ charge ≤ capacity. The only function that changes the charge is draw, which rejects negative inputs and clamps at zero, so the invariant is preserved after every call. The getters are const because they only read the state. For example, a 2000 mAh battery drawing 500 mA for 1.5 hours ends with 2000 − 750 = 1250 mAh, or 62.5%.

