The following function leaks memory. Explain why, and rewrite it twice: once using delete correctly and once without any manual memory management.
double average(int n) {
    double* v = new double[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { v[i] = i; }
    double s = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { s += v[i]; }
    return s / n;
}

questions.modelAnswer:
Each call allocates n doubles on the heap with new[], but nothing frees them; when the function returns, the local pointer v is destroyed, so the program has no way left to release the block and it leaks. Calling the function in a loop would steadily consume memory.
Fix 1:
double average(int n) {
    double* v = new double[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { v[i] = i; }
    double s = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { s += v[i]; }
    delete[] v;
    return s / n;
}
The array form delete[] must be used because the memory came from new[].
Fix 2:
double average(int n) {
    std::vector<double> v(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { v[i] = i; }
    double s = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { s += v[i]; }
    return s / n;
}
The vector releases its memory in its destructor when it goes out of scope (RAII), even if the function returns early. Both versions still need n > 0 to avoid dividing by zero.

int count_nodes(const TreeNode* t) {
    if (t == nullptr) { return 0; }
    return 1 + count_nodes(t->left) + count_nodes(t->right);
}

int height(const TreeNode* t) {
    if (t == nullptr) { return 0; }
    return 1 + std::max(height(t->left), height(t->right));
}
Inserting 8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14 gives root 8; 3 goes left with children 1 and 6; 10 goes right with right child 14. There are 6 nodes, and the longest root-to-leaf paths (8-3-1, 8-3-6, 8-10-14) each contain 3 nodes, so the height is 3. Both functions visit every node once, so they are O(n).

