int power(int b, int e) {
    if (e == 0) { return 1; }
    if (e % 2 == 0) {
        int half = power(b, e / 2);
        return half * half;
    }
    return b * power(b, e - 1);
}
Trace: power(2,10) needs power(2,5); power(2,5) = 2 × power(2,4); power(2,4) needs power(2,2); power(2,2) needs power(2,1); power(2,1) = 2 × power(2,0) = 2 × 1 = 2. Unwinding: power(2,2) = 2 × 2 = 4, power(2,4) = 16, power(2,5) = 32, power(2,10) = 32 × 32 = 1024. The exponent is at least halved every two calls (an odd step makes it even), so the number of calls is O(log e), compared with O(e) for multiplying b by itself e times. It is important to store half rather than writing power(b, e/2) * power(b, e/2), which would make two calls per level and lose the improvement.

