    if (!infile.is_open()) {
        std::cout << "cannot open " << filename << std::endl;
        return rs;
    }
    Resistor r;
    while (infile >> r.id >> r.ohms) {
        if (r.ohms > 0) {
            rs.push_back(r);
        }
    }
    return rs;
}
The extraction is the loop condition, so the loop stops cleanly at end of file or at the first unreadable record.

(b)
double series(const std::vector<Resistor>& rs) {
    double total = 0;
    for (const Resistor& r : rs) { total += r.ohms; }
    return total;
}

double parallel(const std::vector<Resistor>& rs) {
    double g = 0;
    for (const Resistor& r : rs) { g += 1.0 / r.ohms; }
    return 1.0 / g;
}
In parallel the conductances add, so the equivalent resistance is the reciprocal of the sum of reciprocals. Writing 1.0 rather than 1 guarantees floating-point division. Values are already known to be positive, so there is no division by zero for a non-empty vector.

(c)
bool by_value(const Resistor& a, const Resistor& b) { return a.ohms < b.ohms; }
...
std::vector<Resistor> rs = read_resistors("parts.txt");
if (rs.empty()) { return 1; }
std::sort(rs.begin(), rs.end(), by_value);
std::cout << "series " << series(rs) << std::endl;
std::cout << "parallel " << parallel(rs) << std::endl;
std::cout << "smallest " << rs.front().id << std::endl;
Checking for an empty vector first matters, because front() on an empty vector is undefined behaviour and parallel() would divide by zero.

(d) Series: 100 + 220 + 470 = 790 Ω. Parallel: 1/100 + 1/220 + 1/470 = 0.0100 + 0.004545 + 0.002128 = 0.016673 S, so R = 1/0.016673 ≈ 59.98 Ω (the program prints 59.9768 with default stream precision). Smallest: R1. The program therefore prints series 790, parallel 59.9768, smallest R1. A parallel combination is always smaller than the smallest resistor, which is a useful sanity check (59.98 < 100).
Testing: compare with hand calculations for simple cases: two 100 Ω resistors (series 200, parallel 50); a single resistor (both results equal its value). Edge cases: a missing file (error message, non-zero exit), an empty file (no crash, early return), a line with a negative or zero value (skipped), and a malformed line such as R4 abc (reading stops; using getline and a stringstream would let the program skip just that line instead). Running under an address sanitiser or debugger checks there is no out-of-range access.

(a)
class IntSet {
public:
    IntSet() : root(nullptr), count(0) {}
    ~IntSet() { destroy(root); }
    IntSet(const IntSet&) = delete;
    IntSet& operator=(const IntSet&) = delete;
    void insert(int k) { root = insert(root, k); }
    bool contains(int k) const;
    int size() const { return count; }
    int height() const { return height(root); }
private:
    struct Node { int key; Node* left; Node* right; };
    Node* root;
    int count;
    Node* insert(Node* t, int k);
    static int height(const Node* t);
    static void destroy(Node* t);
};
The nodes are private so users cannot break the BST property; they only see set operations (abstraction and encapsulation). The class owns heap memory through raw pointers. The compiler-generated copy constructor would copy root and count only, a shallow copy, so two IntSets would share the same nodes; when both are destroyed each would delete them, a double delete, and changing one would change the other. So copying is disabled with = delete, or a deep copy (recursively cloning every node) plus a matching assignment operator must be written: the rule of three.

(b)
IntSet::Node* IntSet::insert(Node* t, int k) {
    if (t == nullptr) {
        count++;
        return new Node{k, nullptr, nullptr};
    }
    if (k < t->key) { t->left = insert(t->left, k); }
    else if (k > t->key) { t->right = insert(t->right, k); }
    return t;
}
Equal keys fall through both tests and are ignored, which gives set semantics; count only rises when a node is created.

bool IntSet::contains(int k) const {
    const Node* t = root;
    while (t != nullptr) {
        if (k == t->key) { return true; }
        t = (k < t->key) ? t->left : t->right;
    }
    return false;
}

(c)
int IntSet::height(const Node* t) {
    if (t == nullptr) { return 0; }
    return 1 + std::max(height(t->left), height(t->right));
}
void IntSet::destroy(Node* t) {
    if (t == nullptr) { return; }
    destroy(t->left);
    destroy(t->right);
    delete t;
}
Destroy is a post-order traversal: both subtrees are freed before the node whose pointers lead to them.

(d) The tree has root 50; 30 (children 20, 40) on the left; 70 (children 60, 80) on the right. The second 30 is a duplicate and is ignored. size() = 7, height() = 3, contains(40) = true (printed as 1), contains(45) = false (0): the search goes 50 → 30 → 40 → right child nullptr. This is a perfectly balanced tree, so every operation takes at most 3 steps, about log₂ 7.
Inserting 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in order makes each new key the right child of the previous one, a chain with height 5. Search and insertion now take time proportional to n, so the structure has degraded to O(n), no better than a linked list. For large sorted inputs this is a serious problem, and the recursive insert could even overflow the stack. Fixes include self-balancing trees such as red-black or AVL trees (std::set and std::map use a balanced tree, typically red-black, guaranteeing O(log n)), or shuffling input before insertion.
